Section: utils
Priority: optional
Description: Desktop glue for the OnePlus Pad 3 (erhai)
 Everything the GNOME desktop needs on top of the base image: bringing up the
 Adreno 830 GMU before session start and a boot-only GPU pin
 (erhai-gpu-pin-once.service, 2.25: the Adreno is pinned for the boot
 bring-up — an unpinned boot could not bring the desktop up — and released to
 runtime-PM 'auto' once gdm runs, the panel is lit and the GPU clock has risen
 above its idle floor; release only, a timeout stays pinned, an external
 display keeps it pinned.  /etc/erhai/gpu-pin.conf MODE=always restores the
 pre-2.25 permanent pin, erhai-gpu-pin.sh, where the GPU never
 runtime-suspends.  2.26: the pin taken FOR an external DP monitor is handed
 back when that monitor is unplugged (through 'erhai-gpu-pin-once.sh unpin
 ext-detach', so the state file records it), covering both a monitor attached
 later and one attached at boot (state 'kept external'), so a monitor cannot
 leave the GPU pinned for the rest of the boot), and — 2.28 — the GPU WEDGE
 WATCHDOG of 2.27 (erhai-gpu-watch.service) is REMOVED AGAIN by owner decision
 (2026-10-24): it could not clear the real Waydroid wedge — a GPU reset cannot
 retire a submit held by an unreaped zombie — so the pad relies on the kernel fix
 instead of killing clients (kernel +b33: a PINNED Adreno can be recovered
 again, because a8xx_recover() lifts the runtime-PM block that
 power/control=on sets and that block made "cx gdsc didn't collapse" loop
 forever; a6xx_destroy() also frees the pwrup reglist BO that a reload leaked).
 An upgrade from 2.27/2.28 stops, disables and deletes the retired unit, script
 and conffile, so no watcher process is left anywhere,
 boot-time GL environment selection
 (erhai-desktop-gl), power-key REAL panel power cycle
 (erhai-power-toggle + erhai-power-watch.py -> erhai-panel-power.sh: parks the
 GPU then tears the internal DPU/DSI panel down via gnome-shell SetActive, so
 compiles/downloads/external-display apps keep running while the panel sleeps;
 backlight-only toggle while a DP-1 external monitor is attached; GNOME's
 "Suspend" wired to the proven manual-wake s2idle harness via a
 systemd-suspend.service ExecStart override -> erhai-suspend-gnome.sh ->
 erhai-suspend-s2idle.sh (display-off-first + WLAN quiesce + full restore;
 short power press wakes, long press powers off; auto-sleep stays off)),
 pogo keyboard attach/detach -> OSK on/off (erhai-pogo-osk +
 erhai-pogo-osk.py), folio folded onto the screen -> panel backlight
 off/on (erhai-folio-gate.service starts/stops erhai-folio-fold.py with the
 keyboard attach state; erhai-folio-calibrate + erhai-folio-probe.py pick the
 fold signal and write /etc/erhai/folio-fold.conf), the shared backlight
 on/off/toggle script both features use (erhai-display-toggle.sh, which also
 plays a panel-off click on every blank and a panel-on click on every restore
 through the active session via pw-play; /etc/erhai/display.conf can mute or
 replace either), ktz8868
 backlight display toggle, USB-C host charging while acting as data
 host (erhai-host-charge), pogo keyboard activation helper (CLI only -- the
 app-grid launcher was removed 2026-10-17),
 Novatek NT36536 touchscreen bring-up before GDM (erhai-touchscreen +
 touch-calibration udev rule), dconf defaults, and the desktop-users /
 user-groups helpers used by image presets.
 Fixed login-screen policy (2026-09-22): the internal panel comes up at 50 % of
 max_brightness at boot (erhai-backlight.service) and is re-pinned to 50 % for
 every GDM greeter session, i.e. the login screen, by
 erhai-greeter-brightness.py (a logind watcher; user sessions are never
 touched).  GNOME's own ambient-light auto-brightness is forced OFF as a
 GSettings DEFAULT (99-erhai-no-gsd-ambient.gschema.override) because the
 greeter's dconf can end up unreadable, in which case only a default override
 reaches it - that was the login screen crashing to its minimum level.  The
 greeter also gets session-name='gnome-login' (greeter dconf seed +
 /usr/share/gdm/dconf/95-erhai-greeter + /etc/dconf/profile/gdm) and a greeter
 .desktop that pins --session=gnome-login, so a dconf-less greeter can no longer
 come up as a FULL UBUNTU DESKTOP.
 Ships the FIXED gjs-osk on-screen keyboard as a system-wide GNOME Shell
 extension (/usr/share/gnome-shell/extensions/gjsosk@vishram1123.com/), copied
 from the image overlay on every build so the deb and a flashed image can never
 diverge.  Its folio gate (pogoGate.js + the [erhai] hunks in extension.js)
 keeps the OSK from coming up by itself while the pogo keyboard is attached: no
 touch/tap auto-show, no bottom-edge swipe, no restore at login, an
 automatically opened keyboard is retracted on the attach edge, an external
 keyboard-visible=true is forced back to false, and the on-screen CLOSE key
 clears the sticky keyboard-visible flag through the extension object.  It also
 sends modifier+key combinations for the NON-character keys and for HELD
 modifiers (2026-10-06): tapping Shift then Enter sends Shift+Enter (likewise
 arrows/Esc/Tab/Space), and holding Ctrl/Alt/Super with one finger while tapping
 another key sends Ctrl/Alt/Super+key - a held modifier is applied to that key
 and is not left latched behind.  The
 indicator / quick-settings toggle and the `opened` command key still work, so
 the OSK stays one deliberate tap away.  Nothing in it loads in the greeter
 (no session-modes), which keeps GNOME's built-in OSK there.  A USER-LEVEL copy
 of the same uuid takes precedence in gnome-shell (the user data dir is
 collected first and the later system copy is skipped as "already installed"),
 so a device that has one in ~/.local/share/gnome-shell/extensions/ keeps
 loading that one.
 Battery-backed clock (2026-09-29): the PMK8550 PMIC RTC is a 1 Hz
 free-running counter with no wall-clock epoch of its own (and this firmware's
 EFI "RTC" answers with an invalid date), which is why the pad used to boot at
 1970 and only got the right time once WiFi/NTP was up.  Kernel +b8 gives the
 RTC an "offset" nvmem cell (PMIC SDAM 0x7150) and erhai-rtc-sync.py stores
 system_time - counter into it once at boot and hourly
 (erhai-rtc-sync.service + erhai-rtc-sync.timer), so the next boot is right
 from second zero - the same value Android's time_daemon keeps in
 /mnt/vendor/persist/time/ats_1.  The helper refuses to store an implausible
 clock and exits 0 on a kernel without the cell (pre-+b8), so it is harmless
 everywhere.  Audit/seed the cell by hand with workspace tmp/rtc-offset-probe.py.
 Bypass charging (2026-09-30): a GNOME Quick Settings switch
 (erhai-bypass@erhai.local) that stops the battery charging while a charger is
 attached, so the tablet runs from the adapter alone (measured live: pack
 3.93 A -> 0.117 A = 0.49 W with the switch on, back to 1.74 A with it off).
 The switch starts erhai-bypass-charge@<on|off>.service, which polkit lets an
 active local user start without a prompt (50-erhai-bypass-charge.rules); the
 helper /usr/local/sbin/erhai-bypass-charge.sh holds the ADSP battery-psy
 charge-enable property (BATT_CHG_EN = 24, opcode 0x31) down, parks the
 in-kernel max_soc limiter (it drives the same property, so it must stand down)
 and applies a 10 % low-SoC safety floor; erhai-bypass-charge.service applies
 and holds the persisted choice from /etc/default/erhai-charge at boot.
 Since 2.6 it ALSO parks the SC8547A SuperVOOC charge pump (fastchg_deliver /
 fastchg_auto on the *-006f master debugfs) while bypass holds: the CP is a
 direct 2:1 path to the cell driven by the AP kernel, which the ADSP
 charge-enable inhibit cannot reach, so a genuine SuperVOOC brick kept charging
 straight through bypass until the CP itself was stopped (2026-10-03).  On `off`
 it gives the CP back and restarts the VOOC PHY so the ladder re-identifies.
 Since 2.7 the IN-KERNEL CHARGE LIMIT engages the same full bypass at the cap:
 erhai_otg_glink's max_soc (default 80 %) only stopped the ADSP charger, which
 on this hardware left the pad running down on battery (the erhai_pps stall
 watchdog re-injected current, and the SC8547A pump charged through the ADSP
 inhibit).  While the pack sits at the limit the holder now parks the stall
 watchdog AND the charge pump and releases both below the limit (release band
 ERHAI_BYPASS_HYST, default 3 %), so a reached charge limit means "tablet on
 adapter power, pack held" instead of "charging off, battery draining".  The
 kernel limiter keeps owning BATT_CHG_EN (still exactly one writer); the
 augmentation can be disabled with ERHAI_BYPASS_AUTO_LIMIT=0.  `erhai-bypass-
 charge.sh limit [n]` reads/sets the limit.  No kernel change is needed.
 Since 2.8 the UBUNTU DOCK SCROLLS WITH A FINGER: a patched dash.js adds a
 Clutter.PanGesture to the dock's scroll view (capture phase) so a quick swipe
 scrolls the app icons with a kinetic glide, while a press-and-hold still
 starts an icon reorder drag and mouse/trackpad drags are untouched.
 ubuntu-dock@ubuntu.com is a GNOME SESSION-MODE extension, so a per-user
 override is refused and the patched file must sit at its system path, which
 belongs to gnome-shell-ubuntu-extensions; the deb ships it under a private
 name (usr/share/oneplus-erhai-desktop/ubuntu-dock-dash.js) and DEBIAN/postinst
 installs it with dpkg-divert (Ubuntu's file kept as dash.js.erhai-orig), so it
 is not a dpkg file conflict and an Ubuntu upgrade cannot clobber it; postrm
 restores Ubuntu's file on removal.  The package README documents it.
 Re-login needed (GNOME cannot reload extension code).
 Since 2.9 the DESKTOP ICONS NG (DING) EXTENSION OPENS ITS CONTEXT MENUS WITH A
 LONG FINGER TAP: DING (ding@rastersoft.com) draws the desktop background and
 icons in its own gjs process and opened both context menus only on a secondary
 (mouse) button press (button == 3), while a touch tap or long tap arrives as an
 emulated PRIMARY (button 1) press, so a long tap did nothing.  A touch-only
 shim now opens the same menu when a finger is held still for 500 ms over the
 desktop or an icon (cancelled if the finger moves more than 14 px; only events whose device source
 is Gdk.InputSource.TOUCHSCREEN are affected, so mouse and trackpad behaviour is
 untouched and a held mouse button does NOT open the menu).  Like the dock patch, the files belong to gnome-shell-ubuntu-extensions,
 so the patched app/desktopGrid.js, app/desktopManager.js and
 app/desktopIconItem.js ship under /usr/share/oneplus-erhai-desktop/ding-touch/
 and DEBIAN/postinst installs them with dpkg-divert (Ubuntu's files kept as
 *.erhai-orig); postrm restores them.  This is NOT reachable through GNOME's
 built-in "Simulated Secondary Click" accessibility setting: that is implemented
 in Mutter/Clutter for pointer buttons only, and Mutter's touch path emits
 CLUTTER_TOUCH_* events, never a button press.  Re-login (or `pkill -f ding.js`)
 needed to load the patched code.
 Since 2.11 that long press is a SINGLE-FINGER gesture and it is cancelled when
 the compositor cancels the touch.  The 500 ms timer used to be armed on the
 emulated button-1 press of ANY touch, which is indistinguishable with one or
 with three fingers down, so three fingers resting on the glass - or a slow
 3-finger workspace swipe, whose fingers rest before they move - opened the
 desktop menu 500 ms in; the menu's popup grab then cancelled the shell's
 3-finger workspace gesture (SwipeTracker._cancelPanGesture() ends at
 cancelProgress = the CURRENT workspace), i.e. the swipe snapped back and only
 fast swipes still worked.  DING now watches the raw touch events (adds
 Gdk.EventMask.TOUCH_MASK and handles touch-event: GTK3 delivers one per finger,
 while the emulated button event carries no finger count) and a touch the
 compositor CANCELS because the shell grabbed it for a gesture never comes with
 a button-release - so the cancel must also clear the timer.  A live finger
 count + a "saw more than one finger" latch gate the timer, so the menu opens
 only for a real one-finger long press (or a mouse right-click, which is a
 different code path entirely and untouched).
 Since 2.13 the CHARGE-LIMIT BYPASS RELEASES ONLY A PARK IT RECORDED.  The
 release path decided "something is parked" from the SC8547A pump's own
 fastchg_deliver reading 0 - but 0 is ALSO the driver's normal idle state (no
 (S)VOOC brick attached, or any auto-decline/auto-finish/PHY stop), so the holder
 re-armed the pump and bounced the VOOC PHY (vooc 0 -> 1) on EVERY poll; a PHY
 stop flips the USB D+/D- crossbar, so every attached USB device was dropped and
 re-enumerated once per POLL+1 s, forever (measured 2026-10-18: a Kingston
 DataTraveler cycled every 11 s with no charger even attached).  A limit park is
 now recorded in /run/erhai-charge-limit-parked (written BEFORE the park, so a
 park can never exist without its record) and only a recorded park is released,
 exactly once; the manual switch keeps its own record in
 /etc/default/erhai-charge.  No kernel change is needed.
 Since 2.15 the PANEL BLANK HAS ONE OWNER AT A TIME, and a blanked panel is
 DEAF.  Two features blank this panel - the power button (marker
 /run/erhai-panel-off) and the folio-fold watcher (marker
 /run/erhai-folio-fold-blanked) - and the watcher used to claim ANY blank it saw
 and restore it on unfold: turning the display off with the power button, then
 folding the pogo keyboard up and opening it again, lit the panel back up while
 the power button still held it off (measured 2026-10-22 09:18 off ... 09:35:11
 "UNFOLDED -> backlight on" with /run/erhai-panel-off present), which left the
 power button's own state machine out of sync - its next press only deleted the
 marker and the screen stayed on.  The watcher now claims the blank ONLY when
 the panel was still lit when the fold started, never restores while the power
 button's marker exists (unfold, shutdown and the idle/unconfigured path all
 honour that), and erhai-panel-power.sh skips a blank the folio already holds.
 And the fold used to grab the TOUCH node only, while the folded keyboard lying
 on the glass also makes the panel's PEN node emit frames - live proof
 2026-10-22 09:23:57/09:24:34, DURING a grabbed fold: libinput "event5 -
 NVTCapacitivePen: Ignoring pressure offset greater than 50%" - so those
 reached the compositor as pointer activity and woke the session behind a
 dark screen.  Both panel nodes (NVTCapacitiveTouchScreen + NVTCapacitivePen,
 GRAB_DEVICES to override) are suppressed while folded, and erhai-power-watch.py
 suppresses them for as long as ITS blank lasts too (EVIOCGRAB, released
 before the panel comes back, dies with the process, PANEL_INPUT_GUARD=0 in
 /etc/erhai/display.conf disables) - the power key is never grabbed.  Tests:
 tmp/folio-test/{selftest.sh (26),ownership-test.py (15),
 powerwatch-guard-test.py (16),live-grab-test.py (9, real nodes)}.
 Since 2.16 the PANEL BLANK IS HIGH PRIORITY AND IMMEDIATE.  The owner reported
 that turning the display off "can take several seconds before the screen turns
 off when a process with high CPU load is running", and that a resume from s2idle
 brings the display back faster than the power button does - both were true:
   * ORDER - the old sequence was park GPU -> SetActive true -> sleep 2 ->
     backlight off, and the park waits for the GPU to runtime-suspend, which does
     NOT happen while the desktop renders (measured >5.5 s idle, 1.7 s under load,
     budget 10 s), so the panel stayed LIT for 2-12 s after the press.  The
     visible blank (erhai-display-toggle.sh off) is now the FIRST action, the
     ownership marker follows, THEN the bounded park (PARK_WAIT, default 1.5 s)
     and the DPU/DSI teardown - the park-before-teardown ordering that fixed the
     POR is unchanged, only its wait is bounded and it no longer delays the dark
     screen.  do_on dropped its sleep 3 + sleep 1: the s2idle harness has always
     restored the lamp back-to-back with SetActive false (STEP6) and that is the
     proven path (the DSI rail is down while the panel is dark, so an early lamp
     shows nothing); ON_SETTLE_MS keeps a knob in case a flash is ever seen.
   * PRIORITY - erhai-power-toggle.service runs at Nice=-20 with CPUWeight/
     IOWeight 10000 and best-effort I/O priority 0, and both panel scripts renice
     themselves to -20 (children inherit) so nothing queues behind a compile;
     erhai-folio-fold.service takes a moderate -5 (it runs a magnetometer stream).
   * FORKS - the compositor call uses setpriv instead of runuser (no PAM round
     trip: 26 ms vs 101 ms under 8 sysbench threads), the click player likewise,
     say()/snap() no longer spawn date/cut/tee/cat/grep/awk (bash builtins +
     redirects), the 50-170 ms dmesg tail moved off the user path, and the
     daemon's per-press survivability line is fire-and-forget.
   Measured after the change: backlight dark 16 ms idle / 47 ms under 8-thread
   load, lamp restored 13 ms / 35 ms, and the whole `off` stays under 0.5 s even
   when the GPU never parks (was 2-12 s).  Tests: tmp/panel-latency/
   {order-test.sh (19 stub-driven ordering + latency + ownership cases),
   probe.sh (per-stage timings, LOAD=1 for the loaded case), measure-e2e.sh
   (real path on demand, clicks muted)}.
 Since 2.17 the GPU IS NEVER PARKED WHILE AN EXTERNAL DISPLAY IS CONNECTED
 (owner: *"Make sure that GPU is NOT parked when external monitor is connected!"*).
 The power-button blank parks the Adreno on purpose (`echo auto`) - it is only
 needed to make the GPU/MMCX collapse safe once the internal DPU is torn down -
 but a DP monitor over USB-C keeps rendering while the panel is off, and this
 pad's policy is that the Adreno stays pinned always-active (erhai-gpu-pin.sh,
 2026-09-13: the unpinned path is where the GMU resume hang and the display POR
 came from).  Three layers now guarantee it: `park_gpu()` REFUSES to park while
 DP-1 reads `connected` (and re-pins instead), both `do_off`/`do_on` external
 fallbacks call `pin_gpu()`, and a new udev rule
 (etc/udev/rules.d/72-erhai-gpu-external.rules) runs
 usr/local/sbin/erhai-gpu-external-pin.sh on every card*-DP-* add/change event,
 which re-pins the GPU when a monitor is attached while the panel is blanked
 (idempotent: it writes only if a DP connector reads `connected` AND the GPU is
 not already `on`, so repeated uevents cost two reads).  The postinst also fires
 a drm trigger so a monitor that is already attached is handled at install time.
 Tests: tmp/panel-latency/gpu-external-test.sh (16 cases: pin/leave/graceful
 read-only/multi-connector/rule-content) + order-test.sh cases G-I (external
 monitor: no park, no compositor teardown, GPU re-pinned).
 Since 2.18 a blank has ONE owner and that is ENFORCED (2026-10-22).  A
 power-button blank was measured being undone from OUTSIDE - the kernel
 re-enabled the DSI panel 12 min into a 51-minute blank (1 s after a folio fold,
 `nvt_ts_panel_power_on`), the session was AWAKE behind the stale marker, and
 from then on every press drove the DPU and the backlight correctly while the
 screen stayed black until a reboot.  erhai-power-watch.py therefore watches two
