Description: install Waydroid with the erhai Mesa driver payload on the OnePlus Pad 3
 This package does the whole Waydroid setup on this tablet: it carries the
 official Waydroid apt repository (key /usr/share/keyrings/waydroid.gpg, deb822
 source /etc/apt/sources.list.d/waydroid.sources) and, right after its own
 installation, runs `apt-get update` + `apt-get install waydroid` in a deferred
 systemd unit (apt holds its lock while a postinst runs, so the install cannot
 happen inline).  The Waydroid icon then shows up and works like upstream.
 .
 Shutting Android down from its own power menu also ends Waydroid now: a small
 watcher (erhai-waydroid-container-guard.service, started/stopped with
 waydroid-container.service) notices that the LXC container exited and stops the
 unit, whose own SIGTERM handler closes the session and the window and tears
 down the network.  Upstream leaves all of that running, so the next icon click
 would attach to a dead session.
 .
 Waydroid's stock vendor image carries a fake "a830" Mesa driver that wedges
 the Adreno a few seconds into every session on this tablet (SM8750, Adreno
 830).  This package ships the five working Mesa shared objects (stripped
 libgallium_dri, vulkan.freedreno, libEGL_mesa, libGLESv1_CM_mesa,
 libGLESv2_mesa), the in-place patcher erhai_vendor_patch.py and a hook that
 runs it from waydroid's OWN image downloader, so the image the user picks in
 Waydroid on first start is patched as it is downloaded -- and so are
 "waydroid upgrade" and OTA image replaces.
 .
 The patcher rewrites the five files inside vendor.img in place (same inode,
 so the SELinux label, mode and owner survive) and grows the image only by the
 blocks it is really short of - normally by nothing, the payload is a net
 shrink. It refuses a mounted image, so a running session is never touched.
 .
 An apt post-invoke hook re-applies the images.py hook if "apt upgrade
 waydroid" overwrites it. Commands: waydroid-erhai-installer.sh
 [setup|status|patch-now|reapply|uninstall].

